# pam_ssoossh for login(1) on a console: a code and a QR code instead of a
# URL.
#
# A getty login has no PAM_RHOST and a tty that is a virtual console or a
# serial line, so mode=auto runs the console flow: the module prints a short
# code and a QR code, and the approver carries either to a phone or laptop.
# `mode=console` is written out below anyway, so the file says what it
# means; `mode=auto` would choose the same.
#
# Two guards are worth having on a console:
#
#   * root should never see an approval prompt -- an ssoossh account that
#     may become root is a decision for the principals map, not for the
#     console, and the pam_succeed_if line below skips the module for root.
#     Replace `user != root` with `user ingroup ssoossh-console` to allow
#     only members of a group instead.
#
#   * pam_securetty.so stays first, as your distribution ships it.
#
# A console login has more time to walk to another device, hence the longer
# timeout. The line still falls through to the password prompt on a denial,
# a timeout, or an unreachable server.
#
# Test on a spare virtual console (Alt-F3, say) with a root shell open on
# another one.

auth    requisite   pam_securetty.so
auth    [success=ignore default=1]  pam_succeed_if.so user != root quiet
auth    sufficient  pam_ssoossh.so server=https://ssoossh.example.com \
                    trusted-ca-file=/etc/ssoossh/ca.pub \
                    principals-map=/etc/ssoossh/principals.yaml \
                    mode=console timeout=120s

# then the distribution's include:
#   Debian, Ubuntu        @include common-auth
#   RHEL, Alma, Rocky     auth substack system-auth
#   Alpine                auth include base-auth
